# Power management — the node has to still be alive in March

Success is "hike in, months unattended" (`plans/P0_HARDENED_NODE.md`). The node is on a
battery pack with no solar in the ordered BOM (`HARDWARE.md` — Unify 910406, **no
solar**). Average current, not peak current, decides whether the node survives the winter.

**Sleep current dominates the budget.** At a 3600 s interval the node is awake for a few
seconds and asleep for essentially the whole hour. A single stray milliamp in sleep
outweighs everything the radio does. Optimize sleep first, always.

## Non-negotiables

| ID | Requirement | Source |
|---|---|---|
| H2 | Deep sleep between cycles; radio sleeps in Class A | `FIRMWARE_SPEC.md` §7 |
| H3 | Brownout: no flash thrash; skip TX if voltage critically low | §7 |
| H5 | Interval and keys survive power loss | §7 |
| — | `Serial.end()` before sleep | see trap below |

## The RAK4631 sleep traps — these are the documented failure modes

1. **UART left open pins current at milliamp scale.** You must `Serial.end()` (and
   `Serial1.end()`) before sleeping, or the node draws ~mA forever and looks "fine" on the
   bench for an hour while quietly dying in the field [CIT-RAK-SLEEP]. `FIRMWARE_SPEC.md` §5
   step 7 makes this explicit. **This is the single most common way this class of node
   fails.**
2. **The RS-485 transceiver must be disabled between polls.** The RAK5802 driver draws
   continuously if left enabled. Enable it in step 2 of the wake cycle, disable before
   sleep.
3. **A USB-attached debug session masks all of it.** Sleep-current measurements taken over
   USB are meaningless. Measure on battery, and say so in `docs/EVIDENCE.md`.
4. **Deep sleep must be System OFF-class, not a busy `delay()`.** A `delay()` loop keeps
   the core clocked [CIT-NRF-POWER]. Retained state that must survive sleep is documented
   in `docs/POWER_BUDGET.md`.

## Wake cycle discipline

Follow `FIRMWARE_SPEC.md` §5 exactly: pet WDT → enable RS-485 → poll RK900 → poll BMS →
build and TX → handle RX1/RX2 → persist → shut peripherals down → sleep.

Every path out of the cycle — including every error and timeout path — must reach the
sleep step. **An early `return` that skips peripheral shutdown is a battery leak**, and it
will not show up in a short bench test. A hung Modbus read must hit the watchdog (H1) and
reset rather than spin: a reset costs one cycle, a spin costs the deployment.

Bounded work only. Modbus transactions cap at 1000 ms with 2 retries (§2.1); a missing
sensor marks failed and the cycle continues (H6, H7). No unbounded waits, anywhere.

## Brownout

Below the critical threshold: **do not transmit and do not write flash.** TX is the
largest current draw and a brownout mid-flash-write corrupts the stored interval and keys
(H3, H5). Log the state, sleep, and re-evaluate next wake. Repeated flash writes during a
sagging battery are the classic way to lose a node permanently.

The pack reports voltage, current, and SoC over the BMS link (§2.2), so the threshold is
measurable — but note the **unresolved sign convention for battery current**, logged in
ADR-0002. Do not write charge/discharge logic against the sign until that ADR is closed.

## Changing anything power-related

Any change to sleep, peripheral enable/disable, timing, or TX behavior must:

1. Cite the manufacturer figure for the claim (`datasheet`, rule 20).
2. Update `docs/POWER_BUDGET.md` with the recalculated average current and projected
   runtime.
3. Record a **measured** sleep current in `docs/EVIDENCE.md` before the change is called
   done. A calculated budget is a hypothesis; only a measurement closes it.
